 * @param pConverterParams specifies how invalid numbers (including empty strings) should be
 * handled.
 * @param pLineReaderParams specifies how special line formats should be treated.
 */
void Load(const std::string& pPath,
 const LabelParams& pLabelParams = LabelParams(),
 const SeparatorParams& pSeparatorParams = SeparatorParams(),
 const ConverterParams& pConverterParams = ConverterParams(),
 const LineReaderParams& pLineReaderParams = LineReaderParams());
​ ```

2. `rapidcsv::no_converter::what`
​ `rapidcsv::no_converter::what()` is the exception message accessor. It’s used when a requested CSV value/type conversion isn’t supported. Calling `what()` returns the fixed string `"unsupported conversion datatype"`; it has no other behavior or parameters.
​ This function is a member of class rapidcsv::no_converter. Its signature is:
​ ```cpp
/**
 * @brief Provides details about the exception
 * @returns an explanatory string
 */
const char* what() const throw() override;
​ ```

3. `rapidcsv::Converter<std::basic_string<char>>::ToVal`
​ `ToVal()` converts a CSV string to a numeric `T`. It’s meant to be specialized/overridden for custom parsing. It uses `stoi/stol/stoll` and range-checks small integer types; out-of-range throws. Example: `Converter<int>::ToVal` in README/ex008.cpp.
